      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ ACT Traps & Pitfalls — Functions & Graphs",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<ul><li><strong>Order of Composition:</strong> \\( (f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x)) \\) means evaluate \\( g(x) \\) FIRST, then substitute the result into \\( f \\). Evaluating outside-in is one of the most common ACT distractor answer choices.</li><li><strong>Domain Restrictions:</strong> Watch for two key restrictions: denominators cannot equal zero (e.g., \\( \\frac{1}{x-3} \\implies x \\neq 3 \\)), and expressions under an even radical cannot be negative (e.g., \\( \\sqrt{2x-8} \\implies 2x-8 \\ge 0 \\implies x \\ge 4 \\)).</li><li><strong>Vertical Line Test vs. Horizontal Line Test:</strong> A vertical line tests whether a graph is a <em>function</em> (each input has at most one output). A horizontal line tests whether a function is <em>one-to-one</em> (has an invertible function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\)).</li><li><strong>Function Transformations:</strong> \\( f(x - c) \\) shifts RIGHT by \\( c \\), while \\( f(x + c) \\) shifts LEFT by \\( c \\) (horizontal operations are counter-intuitive). Vertical shifts \\( f(x) + c \\) go UP, and \\( f(x) - c \\) go DOWN.</li></ul>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "If \\( f(x)=x^{2}-1 \\), find \\( f(3) \\). \\[ 9-1=8. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "If \\( f(x)=2x+1 \\) and \\( g(x)=x^{2} \\), find \\( f(g(2)) \\). <p>\\( g(2)=4 \\), then \\( f(4)=9 \\):</p>\\[ 9. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "If \\(f(x) = x^2 - 1\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x + 3\\), find \\(f(g(1))\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(g(1) = 5\\). \\(f(5) = 25 - 1 = 24\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "What is the domain of the function \\(f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x + 5}}{x - 3}\\)?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "For the radical in the numerator to be defined, the radicand must be non-negative: \\(x + 5 \\ge 0 \\Rightarrow x \\ge -5\\). For the rational expression to be defined, the denominator cannot equal zero: \\(x - 3 \\neq 0 \\Rightarrow x \\neq 3\\). Combining both restrictions gives \\(x \\ge -5\\) and \\(x \\neq 3\\), or in interval notation \\([-5, 3) \\cup (3, \\infty)\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "If \\(f(x) = 3x - 7\\), what is the formula for the inverse function \\(f^{-1}(x)\\)?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Set \\(y = 3x - 7\\). To find the inverse, interchange \\(x\\) and \\(y\\): \\(x = 3y - 7\\). Solve for \\(y\\): \\(x + 7 = 3y \\Rightarrow y = \\dfrac{x + 7}{3}\\). Therefore, \\(f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x + 7}{3}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
